\documentclass{article}
\title{Latency-Aware Scheduling for Stream Processing}
\author{John Smith}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Stream processing systems must react to events in real time. In our design, the user submits a query graph and the client library compiles it into an execution plan. The customer can then monitor progress through a dashboard. When the workload spikes, the scheduler migrates operators so that the user experiences stable latency. A client may also attach priority hints to urgent streams. Our evaluation shows that customer-perceived tail latency improves by 40 percent, and user satisfaction scores rise accordingly. The client API remains backward compatible.
\section{Design}
The scheduler watches per-operator backlog and migrates stateful operators when the backlog exceeds a threshold.
\end{document}
